We know that reactions with positive $\Delta G_R$ are not spontaneous, but can we adjust pressure or temperature to help spontaneity? We will address this matter in the following.

Let us start by addressing the differences between $\Delta G_R$ and $\Delta G^{\circ}_R$, and how these properties relate to equilibrium. $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ depends on pressure, whereas $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$ depends only on temperature. The value of $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$  is directly related to the pressure ratio between reactants and products. Exergonic reactions at standard conditions ($\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$<0) will have larger product pressures than reactant pressures. This is because a negative $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$ will give $K_c$ values larger than one. Differently, endergonic reactions at standard conditions ($\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$>0)  will have smaller product pressures than reactant pressures. This is because a positive $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$ will give $K_c$ values smaller than one. 

Let us now address the impact of pressure on spontaneity. We can think of many different pressure conditions, that is, many different reactant and product pressure combinations. From all those, there are two very special pressure ratios to know. First, a very special pressure ratio would lead to the equilibrium of the sample: these are the \emph{standard pressure conditions}. In these conditions, $\Delta G_R$ will be zero. A second special pressure ratio would be \emph{standard pressure conditions}, in which all pressures are 1atm. In these conditions $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ would be equal than $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$. For all other conditions, $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ will be either positive or negative. 

On one hand, for pressure conditions having more products (and fewer reactants) than in equilibrium, $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ will be positive: the reaction would not be spontaneous towards the right. On the other hand, for pressure conditions having fewer products (and more reactants) than in equilibrium, $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ will be negative: the reaction would be spontaneous towards the right. 

Now, let us address the effect of changing pressure. Increasing the product's pressure will make $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ more positive, whereas increasing the reactant's pressure will make $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ more negative. This is a thermodynamic interpretation of the \emph{Le Ch\^{a}telier principle}: when we have many reactants and few products, the reaction will proceed to the right. Therefore, a very important idea to remember is that adding fewer products and more reactants than at equilibrium will always drive the reaction towards the right, helping spontaneity.

At this point, we have addressed differences between standard and equilibrium pressure conditions, but we have not mentioned the impact of temperature yet. Let us know address the impact of temperature, as $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ is a function of temperature as well. $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$ only depends on temperature. On one hand, for reactions in which $K_c$ is larger than one (exergonic reactions in standard conditions), increasing temperature will favour spontaneity, as $\Delta G_R(P, T)$ overall increases with temperature. On the other hand, for reactions in which $K_c$ is smaller than one (endergonic reactions in standard conditions), increasing temperature will disfavour spontaneity, hence decreasing $\Delta G_R(P, T)$.

In summary, the pressure and temperature conditions are tangled in their dependence on $\Delta G_R(P, T)$, whereas only temperature affects $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$. We can always adjust the pressure ratio to decrease $\Delta G_R(P, T)$, helping spontaneity. Adding more products and fewer reactants than at equilibrium will always help spontaneity. The impact of temperature on equilibrium depends on the sign of $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$: for negative $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$ values increasing temperature helps spontaneity, whereas for positive $\Delta G^{\circ}_R(T)$ values decreasing temperature helps spontaneity.







